+**A forward step is also capped in how fast it may arrive.** Winning does not
+mean arriving in one reading: `ucal wallclock` caps how far a single reading
+may advance past the last one it handed out to one second's worth of ticks.
+Ordinarily that cap is never reached — the fastest caller reads the clock
+fifty milliseconds apart — so it only engages when a correction outruns real
+elapsed time, and a face that would otherwise jump minutes ahead instead
+catches up over the following second or so of readings. The cap never holds
+back the monotonic branch: real elapsed time always gets through, so it slows
+only the *extra* distance a correction is asking for, never the part that
+would have arrived anyway.
